add(slug="what-does-6-7-mean", cat="Numbers & Culture", read=6,
title="What Does “6-7” Mean? How a Number Became a Word of the Year",
desc="The origin, spread and business lessons of the viral 6-7 meme that Dictionary.com named its 2025 Word of the Year.",
related=["percentage-change-calculator","conversion-rate-calculator"],
body="""
<p>For most of history, numbers were things you counted with. In 2025 one pair of digits became something you <em>said</em>. “6-7” — spoken “six seven,” often with a palms-up, see-saw hand gesture — spread from short-form video into school hallways, sports broadcasts and brand social accounts. By late October 2025, Dictionary.com had named it its Word of the Year.</p>
<h2>Where it came from</h2>
<p>The phrase is usually traced to a rap track by the artist Skrilla titled “Doot Doot (6 7)”. The hook was clipped into countless edits, and its association with professional basketball — notably point guard LaMelo Ball, who helped popularise it as a nod to his height — gave it a sports-highlight life of its own. From there it became a Gen Alpha in-joke: shouted whenever the numbers 6 and 7 appear, or for no reason at all.</p>
<h2>Why a meaningless number caught on</h2>
<p>Dictionary.com's lexicographers described the term as part inside joke, part social signal and part performance. That combination explains its spread better than any literal meaning, because it has almost none. Three mechanics are at work:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low cost to join.</strong> Anyone can say two digits. There is no vocabulary to learn and no wrong way to use it.</li>
<li><strong>Built-in triggers.</strong> Numbers are everywhere — page numbers, scores, prices, clocks — so the meme re-surfaces constantly without anyone pushing it.</li>
<li><strong>Group identity.</strong> Using it marks you as in on the joke; confused adults are part of the fun.</li>
</ul>
<p>The publisher's own data showed the term appearing in digital media roughly six times more often in October 2025 than its 2024 average — a classic viral curve.</p>
<h2>What businesses can learn from it</h2>
<p>Brands that tried to borrow the meme had mixed results; teenage audiences notice forced slang immediately. The useful lessons are structural rather than tactical:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Numbers are memorable assets.</strong> Short, rhythmic number strings are easy to say and share. This is why companies pay premiums for numeric phone lines, licence plates and domains.</li>
<li><strong>Triggers beat reach.</strong> A message tied to an everyday cue gets repeated for free. Ask what everyday moment could remind a customer of your product.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the curve.</strong> Viral attention spikes and decays. Use the <a href="../calculators/percentage-change-calculator.html">percentage change calculator</a> on your own traffic to separate a spike from a trend.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Is the number lucky or unlucky?</h2>
<p>Neither, in the meme. In Chinese number symbolism, however, 6 is strongly positive (it sounds like the word for “smooth”), while 7 carries mixed associations. Our <a href="../number-lab.html?n=67">Number Lab</a> breaks down any number by digit, pattern and memorability.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> “6-7” shows that a number does not need meaning to have value — it needs to be easy to say, easy to trigger and fun to share.</div>
""")

add(slug="lucky-numbers-in-business", cat="Numbers & Culture", read=7,
title="Lucky Numbers in Business: Why 8 Sells and 4 Is Avoided",
desc="How cultural number symbolism shapes prices, phone numbers, addresses and domains — with practical rules for global businesses.",
related=["pricing-calculator","unit-price-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Numbers carry meaning far beyond arithmetic. In many East-Asian markets especially, the sound of a digit affects what people will pay for a phone number, which floor they want to live on and even which product price feels right. If you sell internationally, ignoring this is leaving money on the table.</p>
<h2>The homophone principle</h2>
<p>In Mandarin and Cantonese, several digits sound like other words. Those sound-alikes give digits their reputation:</p>
<table><tr><th>Digit</th><th>Sounds like</th><th>Reputation</th></tr>
<tr><td>8 (bā)</td><td>fā — to prosper</td><td>The most prized digit for business</td></tr>
<tr><td>6 (liù)</td><td>liú — to flow, smooth</td><td>Very positive; “everything goes smoothly”</td></tr>
<tr><td>9 (jiǔ)</td><td>jiǔ — long-lasting</td><td>Positive; longevity</td></tr>
<tr><td>4 (sì)</td><td>sǐ — death</td><td>Widely avoided</td></tr>
<tr><td>7 (qī)</td><td>varies</td><td>Mixed: togetherness in some contexts, “gone” in others</td></tr>
<tr><td>5 (wǔ)</td><td>wú — nothing / or “I” (wǒ) in combos</td><td>Neutral to mixed</td></tr></table>
<h2>Real-world money effects</h2>
<p>Premium numbers are traded like collectibles. Phone numbers made entirely of 8s have sold at auction for six-figure sums, and car plates rich in 8s and 6s attract bidding wars in several Asian markets. Buildings in some cities skip the fourth floor, or every floor ending in 4. The opening ceremony of the Beijing Olympics famously began at 8:08 pm on 8 August 2008.</p>
<h2>Popular combinations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>168</strong> — “prosperity all the way”, common in shop names and prices.</li>
<li><strong>518</strong> — “I will prosper”.</li>
<li><strong>58</strong> — read in some contexts as “I prosper”.</li>
<li><strong>520</strong> — sounds like “I love you”; 20 May has become an informal romantic holiday.</li>
<li><strong>666</strong> — “smooth”, and online slang for “impressive”.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Western number psychology</h2>
<p>Western markets have their own quirks: prices ending in 9 or .99 (“charm pricing”) are perceived as cheaper than the next round number, while round prices can signal premium quality. Thirteen is avoided in some building and hotel floor numbering. Seven is broadly considered lucky.</p>
<h2>Practical rules for global sellers</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Audit your prices by market.</strong> A price ending in 4 may underperform in some Chinese-speaking markets; one ending in 8 may outperform.</li>
<li><strong>Choose memorable contact numbers.</strong> Repeated digits and short patterns are easier to recall in any culture.</li>
<li><strong>Test, don't assume.</strong> Symbolism varies by region, generation and dialect. A/B test price endings before standardising.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use the <a href="../number-lab.html">Number Lab</a> to score any phone number, price or domain for memorability and digit symbolism, and the <a href="../calculators/pricing-calculator.html">price-from-margin calculator</a> to make sure a “lucky” price still hits your margin.</p>
<p class="muted">Number symbolism is cultural tradition presented for information, not a prediction of outcomes.</p>
""")

add(slug="compound-interest-explained", cat="Money", read=7,
title="Compound Interest Explained: The Math That Builds (or Destroys) Wealth",
desc="A plain-English guide to compound interest with worked numbers, the Rule of 72, and how it works against you on debt.",
related=["compound-interest-calculator","investment-growth-calculator","credit-card-payoff-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Compound interest means earning returns on your previous returns. It is the reason small, early savings can outgrow large, late ones — and the reason unpaid credit card balances snowball.</p>
<h2>The formula</h2>
<span class="formula">A = P × (1 + r/k)^(k × t)</span>
<p>P is the starting amount, r the annual rate, k the number of compounding periods per year and t the number of years.</p>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>Invest 10,000 at 7% a year for 30 years:</p>
<ul><li>Compounded annually: about <strong>76,122.55</strong></li><li>Compounded monthly: about <strong>81,164.97</strong></li></ul>
<p>Now add 300 a month for the same 30 years. You deposit 108,000 of your own money on top of the original 10,000, and the account grows to roughly <strong>447,000</strong> — around three-quarters of the final balance is growth, not deposits.</p>
<h2>Time beats rate</h2>
<p>Doubling the time horizon usually matters more than squeezing an extra percent of return. Because growth is exponential, the final years add the most money. Starting ten years earlier can be worth more than doubling contributions later.</p>
<h2>The Rule of 72</h2>
<p>Divide 72 by the annual rate to estimate how many years it takes money to double. At 6% that is 12 years; at 9% about 8 years; at 24% (a typical credit card APR range) just 3 years. That last example is why compounding is dangerous on debt.</p>
<h2>Compounding against you</h2>
<p>Credit card interest is usually calculated daily or monthly on the outstanding balance. If your payment barely covers interest, the balance hardly moves. Run your own balance through the <a href="../calculators/credit-card-payoff-calculator.html">credit card payoff calculator</a> to see how a small payment increase cuts months off the timeline.</p>
<h2>Inflation and real returns</h2>
<p>A 7% nominal return in a 3% inflation environment is roughly a 4% real return. For long-range planning, test both nominal and real scenarios with the <a href="../calculators/inflation-calculator.html">inflation calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Checklist</h2>
<ol><li>Start early, even small.</li><li>Automate monthly contributions.</li><li>Increase contributions every year (a step-up).</li><li>Clear high-interest debt before investing aggressively.</li><li>Keep fees low — they compound too.</li></ol>
<p class="muted">Educational content only; not individual financial advice. Returns are not guaranteed.</p>
""")

add(slug="break-even-analysis-guide", cat="Business", read=6,
title="Break-Even Analysis: Know Your Survival Number Before You Launch",
desc="How to calculate break-even units and revenue, read contribution margin, and use the result to set prices.",
related=["break-even-calculator","profit-margin-calculator","pricing-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Every business has a survival number: the sales volume where revenue exactly covers costs. Below it you lose money; above it you profit. Knowing it before launch changes how you price, hire and spend.</p>
<h2>Fixed vs variable costs</h2>
<p><strong>Fixed costs</strong> stay the same regardless of sales — rent, salaries, software subscriptions, insurance. <strong>Variable costs</strong> rise with each unit sold — materials, payment fees, shipping, per-unit commissions.</p>
<h2>Contribution margin</h2>
<p>Each sale contributes <em>price minus variable cost</em> toward fixed costs. That contribution is the engine of break-even.</p>
<span class="formula">Break-even units = Fixed costs ÷ (Price − Variable cost per unit)</span>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>A small online store has 12,000 of monthly fixed costs. It sells a product at 40 with 16 of variable cost. Each unit contributes 24, so it needs <strong>500 units</strong> (20,000 of revenue) a month to break even. The contribution margin ratio is 60%, which also gives break-even revenue directly: 12,000 ÷ 0.60 = 20,000.</p>
<h2>Three levers, ranked</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Price.</strong> Raising the price from 40 to 44 lifts contribution to 28 and cuts break-even to about 429 units — a 14% reduction from a 10% price change.</li>
<li><strong>Variable cost.</strong> Negotiating supplier costs from 16 to 14 cuts break-even to about 462 units.</li>
<li><strong>Fixed cost.</strong> Cutting 1,200 of overhead (10%) brings break-even to 450 units.</li>
</ol>
<p>Price is usually the most powerful lever, which is why discounting can be so destructive: a 10% discount here pushes break-even to about 600 units.</p>
<h2>Adding a profit target</h2>
<p>To earn a target profit, add it to fixed costs. Wanting 6,000 a month of profit in the example requires (12,000 + 6,000) ÷ 24 = 750 units.</p>
<h2>Limits of the model</h2>
<p>Break-even assumes a constant price and variable cost. In reality, volume discounts, seasonal demand and capacity limits bend the line. Treat it as a planning baseline and re-run it whenever costs change.</p>
<p>Try it with your numbers in the <a href="../calculators/break-even-calculator.html">break-even calculator</a>.</p>
""")

add(slug="margin-vs-markup", cat="Business", read=5,
title="Margin vs Markup: The Pricing Mistake That Quietly Kills Profit",
desc="Why a 50% markup is only a 33% margin, with conversion tables and pricing rules.",
related=["markup-calculator","profit-margin-calculator","pricing-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Margin and markup describe the same profit, divided by different numbers. Mixing them up is one of the most common — and most expensive — pricing errors in small business.</p>
<h2>Definitions</h2>
<span class="formula">Markup = Profit ÷ Cost
Margin = Profit ÷ Price</span>
<p>An item costing 60 and selling for 100 has a profit of 40: a <strong>66.7% markup</strong> but a <strong>40% margin</strong>.</p>
<h2>Conversion table</h2>
<table><tr><th>Markup</th><th>Margin</th></tr><tr><td>25%</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>50%</td><td>33.3%</td></tr><tr><td>75%</td><td>42.9%</td></tr><tr><td>100%</td><td>50%</td></tr><tr><td>150%</td><td>60%</td></tr><tr><td>200%</td><td>66.7%</td></tr></table>
<p>Conversions: Margin = Markup ÷ (1 + Markup), and Markup = Margin ÷ (1 − Margin).</p>
<h2>How the mistake happens</h2>
<p>An owner wants a 40% margin, so they add 40% to cost. A 60 cost becomes 84 — but the margin on 84 is only 28.6%. Across a year of sales, that gap can wipe out the entire profit target.</p>
<h2>The correct way to price from a margin target</h2>
<span class="formula">Price = Cost ÷ (1 − Target margin)</span>
<p>For a 40% margin on a 60 cost: 60 ÷ 0.6 = 100.</p>
<h2>Which should you use?</h2>
<ul><li>Use <strong>markup</strong> when quoting from supplier cost (common in retail and trades).</li><li>Use <strong>margin</strong> when reading financial statements, comparing with competitors or setting company targets.</li></ul>
<p>Most important: be explicit. Write “40% gross margin”, never just “40%”.</p>
<p>Calculate both instantly with the <a href="../calculators/markup-calculator.html">markup calculator</a> and the <a href="../calculators/pricing-calculator.html">price-from-target-margin calculator</a>.</p>
""")

add(slug="website-earnings-math", cat="Websites & AI", read=7,
title="How Much Can a Website Earn? Traffic, RPM and Valuation Math",
desc="The equations behind content-site revenue: pageviews, RPM, affiliate funnels, lead value — and how sites are valued at sale.",
related=["adsense-revenue-calculator","website-valuation-calculator","affiliate-earnings-calculator","lead-value-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Website revenue is not mysterious; it is a handful of multiplications. Understanding them tells you which lever to pull next — more traffic, better monetisation or a higher-value audience.</p>
<h2>1. Display advertising</h2>
<span class="formula">Ad revenue = Pageviews ÷ 1,000 × Page RPM</span>
<p>Page RPM (revenue per thousand pageviews) blends ad click-through, advertiser bids and how many ads each page shows. A site with 50,000 monthly pageviews at an 8 RPM earns about 400 a month. RPM varies widely by topic and audience country, so measure your own once live.</p>
<h2>2. Affiliate commissions</h2>
<span class="formula">Earnings = Visitors × CTR × Conversion rate × Order value × Commission</span>
<p>20,000 visitors × 8% click-through × 3% conversion × 120 order value × 8% commission ≈ 461 a month. Compare <em>earnings per 1,000 visitors</em> with your ad RPM to decide which pages should prioritise which model.</p>
<h2>3. Lead generation</h2>
<p>For service niches, leads often beat both. If 15% of leads close on a 1,200 deal, every lead is worth 180. Even ten leads a month can out-earn tens of thousands of ad pageviews.</p>
<h2>4. Direct sponsorships</h2>
<p>Once a site owns an audience, sponsors pay flat fees for placements, newsletters and contest prizes. Sponsorship revenue is less tied to pageview volume and more to audience fit.</p>
<h2>Stacking models</h2>
<p>The strongest sites stack all four: ads on informational pages, affiliates on comparison pages, a lead form where intent is high, and sponsors on the newsletter. Avoid placing ads next to your best-converting lead form — they compete for the same click.</p>
<h2>What a site is worth</h2>
<span class="formula">Value ≈ Average monthly net profit × Multiple</span>
<p>Content sites commonly trade at multiples of monthly profit that depend on age, trend, traffic diversity and owner workload. A site netting 1,500 a month at a 36× multiple is valued around 54,000.</p>
<h2>Growth math</h2>
<p>5% monthly traffic growth compounds to roughly 80% a year. That is why consistent publishing beats sporadic bursts.</p>
<p>Model your own site with the <a href="../calculators/adsense-revenue-calculator.html">ad revenue</a>, <a href="../calculators/affiliate-earnings-calculator.html">affiliate</a>, <a href="../calculators/lead-value-calculator.html">lead value</a> and <a href="../calculators/website-valuation-calculator.html">valuation</a> calculators.</p>
""")

add(slug="ai-costs-for-small-business", cat="Websites & AI", read=7,
title="What AI Really Costs a Small Business: Tokens, Seats and ROI",
desc="A practical framework for budgeting AI tools and APIs, estimating token costs and proving return on investment.",
related=["ai-token-cost-calculator","ai-roi-calculator"],
body="""
<p>AI spending falls into two buckets: <strong>seats</strong> (monthly subscriptions per user) and <strong>usage</strong> (API calls billed per token). Each has a different cost curve, and each needs a different ROI test.</p>
<h2>Seats: predictable, but watch adoption</h2>
<p>A per-user subscription is easy to budget. The risk is paying for licences nobody uses. ROI is driven by three numbers: hours saved per person, the share of people who actually use the tool, and the loaded hourly cost of those people.</p>
<span class="formula">Monthly value = Hours saved/week × People × Adoption × Hourly cost × 52 ÷ 12</span>
<p>Example: 4 hours a week × 5 people × 70% adoption × 45 per hour ≈ 2,730 of monthly value against a 300 tool bill.</p>
<h2>Usage: scalable, but variable</h2>
<p>APIs bill per million tokens, usually with output priced several times higher than input. A token is roughly three-quarters of an English word.</p>
<span class="formula">Cost per request = Input tokens × Input price/1M + Output tokens × Output price/1M</span>
<p>At 800 input and 400 output tokens with prices of 3 and 15 per million, each request costs 0.0084 — about 252 a month at 1,000 requests a day.</p>
<h2>Five ways to cut AI costs</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Trim prompts.</strong> Long system prompts are paid for on every call.</li><li><strong>Cap output length.</strong> Output tokens are the expensive ones.</li><li><strong>Cache repeated context</strong> where your provider supports it.</li><li><strong>Route by difficulty.</strong> Send simple classification to a smaller, cheaper model.</li><li><strong>Batch non-urgent jobs</strong> if your provider discounts batch processing.</li></ol>
<h2>Proving ROI</h2>
<p>Measure before and after: tickets closed per hour, articles drafted per week, time to first response. Saved time only becomes money when it is redeployed to revenue work or avoids a hire — otherwise report it as capacity.</p>
<h2>Hidden costs</h2>
<p>Include human review time, integration work, data-handling compliance and the cost of errors. A realistic model beats an optimistic one when you present the budget.</p>
<p>Run your numbers in the <a href="../calculators/ai-token-cost-calculator.html">AI token cost</a> and <a href="../calculators/ai-roi-calculator.html">AI ROI</a> calculators.</p>
""")

add(slug="startup-runway-guide", cat="Business", read=6,
title="Runway, Burn Rate and How Long Your Cash Really Lasts",
desc="Calculate gross and net burn, model revenue growth, and decide when to fundraise or cut.",
related=["startup-runway-calculator","cac-ltv-calculator","break-even-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Runway is the number of months a company can operate before cash runs out. It is the single most important number for an early-stage founder because every other plan depends on it.</p>
<h2>Gross burn vs net burn</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Gross burn</strong> = total monthly operating expenses.</li><li><strong>Net burn</strong> = expenses minus revenue — the actual monthly cash drain.</li></ul>
<span class="formula">Simple runway = Cash ÷ Net burn</span>
<p>With 120,000 in the bank, 15,000 of expenses and 3,000 of revenue, net burn is 12,000 and simple runway is 10 months.</p>
<h2>Adding growth</h2>
<p>If revenue grows 5% a month, each month's net burn shrinks slightly and runway stretches to about 11 months. Growth helps, but at small revenue bases it rarely saves a company on its own — cost discipline does.</p>
<h2>The fundraising clock</h2>
<p>Raising capital commonly takes several months from first meeting to money in the bank. Many advisors suggest starting with at least 6–9 months of runway remaining so you are not negotiating from desperation.</p>
<h2>Default alive or default dead?</h2>
<p>Ask: if nothing changes — same costs, same growth — does revenue overtake expenses before cash runs out? If yes, you are “default alive”. If not, you need to raise, cut or change the growth rate.</p>
<h2>Extending runway</h2>
<ol><li>Cut the largest fixed costs first; small cuts rarely move runway.</li><li>Raise prices for new customers.</li><li>Collect receivables faster and offer annual prepayment discounts.</li><li>Pause experiments that have not shown signal in 90 days.</li></ol>
<p>Model it with the <a href="../calculators/startup-runway-calculator.html">runway calculator</a> and stress-test your unit economics with the <a href="../calculators/cac-ltv-calculator.html">CAC & LTV calculator</a>.</p>
""")

add(slug="unit-economics-for-founders", cat="Business", read=6,
title="Unit Economics: The 7 Numbers Every Founder Should Know",
desc="CAC, LTV, payback, churn, gross margin, conversion rate and break-even — what they mean and healthy ranges.",
related=["cac-ltv-calculator","conversion-rate-calculator","lead-value-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Revenue tells you how big a business is. Unit economics tell you whether growing it is a good idea. These seven numbers fit on one page and answer most investor and strategy questions.</p>
<h2>1. Customer acquisition cost (CAC)</h2>
<p>Sales and marketing spend divided by new customers in the same period. Include salaries and tools, not just ad spend.</p>
<h2>2. Gross margin</h2>
<p>Revenue minus direct costs of delivering the product, as a percentage of revenue. It sets how much of each sale is available to repay CAC.</p>
<h2>3. Churn</h2>
<p>The share of customers lost per month. Average lifetime is roughly 1 ÷ monthly churn: 4% churn implies about 25 months.</p>
<h2>4. Lifetime value (LTV)</h2>
<span class="formula">LTV = (Monthly revenue per customer × Gross margin) ÷ Monthly churn</span>
<p>30 a month at 80% margin with 4% churn gives an LTV of 600.</p>
<h2>5. LTV : CAC</h2>
<p>A common benchmark for subscription businesses is 3× or better. Below 1× you lose money on every customer; far above 5× may mean you are under-investing in growth.</p>
<h2>6. CAC payback</h2>
<p>Months of gross profit needed to recover CAC. With CAC of 150 and 24 of monthly gross profit per customer, payback is 6.25 months.</p>
<h2>7. Conversion rate</h2>
<p>The share of visitors or leads who become customers. Improving conversion lowers CAC without touching the ad budget.</p>
<h2>Putting it together</h2>
<p>Track these monthly, by cohort and by channel. Blended averages hide weak channels. When one number moves, re-run the others: a churn increase from 4% to 5% cuts LTV by 20%.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="../calculators/cac-ltv-calculator.html">CAC & LTV calculator</a> and <a href="../calculators/conversion-rate-calculator.html">conversion rate calculator</a> with your own data.</p>
""")

add(slug="domain-investing-by-the-numbers", cat="Websites & AI", read=6,
title="Domain Investing by the Numbers: Renewals, Sell-Through and Real Returns",
desc="The portfolio math of buying, holding and selling domain names — and why numeric domains are a category of their own.",
related=["domain-flip-calculator","website-valuation-calculator"],
body="""
<p>Domain investing looks like buying low and selling high. In practice it is a portfolio business where holding costs, sell-through rate and commissions decide profit far more than any single sale.</p>
<h2>The single-name view</h2>
<span class="formula">Profit = Sale price × (1 − Commission) − (Purchase + Renewals × Years)</span>
<p>Buy at 500, renew at 12 a year for three years, sell for 3,500 through a marketplace charging 15%: net proceeds 2,975, cost basis 536, profit 2,439.</p>
<h2>The portfolio view</h2>
<p>Most names in any portfolio never sell. If you hold 500 names at 12 a year, renewals cost 6,000 annually. With a low single-digit annual sell-through rate, every sale must also pay for the names that did not sell. That is why experienced investors price fewer names higher rather than many names cheaply.</p>
<h2>Why numeric domains are different</h2>
<p>Short numeric .com domains form a distinct market. They are language-neutral, easy to type on any keyboard and attractive in markets where number symbolism matters. Supply of short numeric strings is fixed, which supports pricing for the shortest lengths.</p>
<h2>Developing vs parking</h2>
<p>A developed website can turn a holding cost into an income stream. Even modest ad, affiliate or lead revenue can cover renewals and creates a traffic history that some buyers value. A useful site also demonstrates a name's potential to an end user.</p>
<h2>Key metrics to track</h2>
<ul><li>Total annual renewal cost</li><li>Annual sell-through rate</li><li>Average sale price and median sale price</li><li>Inquiries per year and inquiry-to-sale conversion</li><li>Revenue from developed names</li></ul>
<p>Model a flip with the <a href="../calculators/domain-flip-calculator.html">domain flip calculator</a> and a developed site with the <a href="../calculators/website-valuation-calculator.html">website valuation calculator</a>.</p>
""")
